Component: cSID (Commodore 64 SID chip, .sid/.rsid)
Author: Mihaly Horvath ("Hermit"), 2016-2017. http://hermit.sidrip.com
        Vendored from https://github.com/mlund/csid (the cycle-based csid.c),
        at musicplayer/src/plugins/csidplugin/csid/csid_engine.c.
License: WTFPL -- "Do what the fuck you want with this code, but please mention
         me as its original author." Fully permissive; no copyleft obligation.
Notice: A from-scratch 6581/8580 + 6510 + PSID player. It shares NO code with
        VICE, reSID or libsidplayfp, and needs no Commodore ROM images, which is
        what makes C64 SID playback possible in the Mac App Store variant. The
        emulation is vendored unchanged apart from library-isation (SDL and
        main() removed, file-scope symbols made static, an unsequenced-evaluation
        fix in the operand fetch); rendered output was verified bit-identical to
        the pristine upstream. Attribution to Hermit is a licence condition and
        is carried in README.md and data/misc/Credits.rtf.
Coverage: cSID does not emulate enough of a real C64 (KERNAL banked in, CIA and
        raster running) to run an RSID whose header play address is $0000 and
        which therefore installs its own IRQ/NMI handler. Measured over the whole
        collection: 3708 RSIDs and 109 PSIDs carry play=$0000; 2382 of them are
        silent on EVERY subtune, 1332 play normally and 103 are mixed. The silent
        ones are listed in data/misc/csid_silent_sids.txt and dropped from the
        catalog in the Mac App Store build only, so no unplayable row is shown.
        The plus build plays them through VICE and is unaffected.

Component: Compute!'s Sidplayer sequencer (.mus / .str) -- musplugin,
           shown in the app as "ChipMachine Clean Room SIDPlayer"
Author: Original code written for this project (clean room). The FORMAT is Craig
        Chamberlin's Compute!'s Sidplayer, published by COMPUTE! Publications.
        Implemented from two freely-distributed format descriptions shipped in
        the Compute's Gazette SID Collection: MUS_format_A.txt by Peter Weighill
        -- which states it was produced by hex-editing editor output "without any
        reference to the book written by Craig Chamberlin" -- and
        MUS_format_B.txt by Dick Thornton.
License: Our implementation is original work under this project's terms. The two
         format descriptions are freely distributed within the CGSC archive.
Notice: CLEAN ROOM. No code was taken or consulted from libsidplayfp, sidplayfp
        or JSIDPlay2 (all GPL), nor from any disassembly of the original 1980s
        Sidplayer routine. Behaviour the documents leave unspecified was derived
        by black-box observation of emulator register OUTPUT only. This is what
        allows Compute!'s Sidplayer to ship in the Mac App Store variant, where
        the GPL VICE bridge cannot. The SID chip emulation underneath is cSID
        (WTFPL, see above). See musplugin/README.md for the full derivation.
        METHOD, for the record: the only thing observed was the stream of SID
        register writes an emulator produces while playing a file -- address,
        value and cycle timestamp. That output was compared against ours on a
        1 ms grid and the sequencer's rules were fitted to the difference. No
        player code, symbol, table or disassembly listing was read at any point;
        where a rule could not be recovered from output alone it was left
        unimplemented and recorded as a known gap rather than guessed at from
        source. Thirteen behaviours the documents do not specify were recovered
        this way (tick rate, note table rounding, default tempo, startup delay,
        repeat-count semantics, explicit note lengths, zero-length events,
        filter-tracking, filter register scope, the modulation schedule, the two
        sweeps and portamento).
        VALIDATION: all 16,601 .mus files in the CGSC archive render -- zero
        crashes, zero hangs, zero parse failures; 9 render silent and all 9 are
        silent in the reference too. Register agreement with the reference has a
        median of 98.5% across the full corpus.

